          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Yang-Mills Mass Gap problem
            is an open conceptual problem in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
            (cf. general
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gaps"} spectral gaps
            of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+systems"} quantum systems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadronic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            at ordinary (non-excessive)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperatures"} temperatures
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion+fields"} fermion fields
            (such as for
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed "-less" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluons"} gluons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperature"} temperature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            is thought to exhibit a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark-gluon+plasma"} quark-gluon plasma
            well described by these degrees of freedom.
          %p
            But at comparatively smaller
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperature"} temperature
            it is observed in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            as well as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+QCD"} lattice QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+experiment"} computer experiment
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            exhibits
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            in the form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadrons"} hadrons
            (including, notably,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/protons"} protons
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrons"} neutrons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
          %p
            The existence of massive
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            in low-energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            is thus well-established
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} pincludehenomenologically
          %p
            The
            %em mass gap problem
            is the problem in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            to demonstrate theoretically (i.e. not just by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+QCD"} computer simulation
            = succeed "-phenomenon" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion+fields"} fermion fields
            in general.
          %p
            This issue is well and widely known in the
            = succeed "-community," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kutschke96"} Kutschke 96, Section 3.1
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#INFN15"} INFN 15
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed "-community" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            %a{:href => "http://www.claymath.org/"} Clay Mathematics Institute
            declared the problem to be one in a list of “
            %a{:href => "http://www.claymath.org/millennium-problems"}> Millennium Problems
            ”, see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#JaffeWitten00"} Jaffe-Witten 2000
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Douglas04"} Douglas 04
          %p In contrast to the other Millennium problems, the Yang-Mills mass gap problem is more wide-ranging, since the issue is not primarily to perform a complicated deduction in an otherwise well-understood theoretical framework, but to establish such a theoretical backdrop in the first place:
          %p
            Namely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            is “strongly coupled”, meaning that common methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            do not apply: What is needed to even state the mass gap problem with precision is first a formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            – which is generally a wide open problem for interacting field theories in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensions"} dimensions
          %p
            While there exist several models for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+hadrodynamics"} quantum hadrodynamics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+perturbation+theory"}> chiral perturbation theory
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmions"} Skyrmions
            %em ad hoc
            phenomenological models not actually derived from a “microscopic” theory like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            and hence tend to assume the mass gap more than explaining it.
          %p
            A more fundamental approach to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} YM
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrme
            model with its predicted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/masses"} masses
            appears as an emergent effect.
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gap"} spectral gap
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"} mass term
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gapped+Hamiltonian"} gapped Hamiltonian
          %h2#references References
          %h3#ReferencesMassGapProblem The mass gap problem
          %p
            Survey and problem description in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.claymath.org/"}> Clay Mathematics Institute
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.claymath.org/millennium/yang-mills-the-maths-gap"} Yang-Mills &amp; The Mass Gap
            %li#JaffeWitten00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Jaffe"}> Arthur Jaffe
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Yang-Mills theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Jaffe"} A. Jaffe
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Wiles"} A. Wiles
                (eds.):
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Millennium Prize Problems
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/MPRIZE"} ISBN:978-1-4704-7460-7
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.claymath.org/library/monographs/MPPc.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.claymath.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/yangmills.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.arthurjaffe.com/Assets/pdf/QuantumYangMillsWebRevised.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/JaffeWitten-QuantumYangMills.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Atiyah2000
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The millennium prize problems II
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.msri.org/realvideo/ln/hosted/cmi/2000/cmiparis/index-tate.html"} CMI Millennium Event at Collège de France
                (24-25 May 2000) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.claymath.org/lectures/the-millennium-prize-problems-ii/"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY"} YT
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=1764"} 29:40
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2420"} 40:20
              What is the future of this problem &amp;lbrack;Yang-Mills &amp; mass-gap&amp;rbrack; in physics, what could happen?
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2424"} 40:24
              This is now my personal guess or, if you like, hints &amp;lbrack;or&amp;rbrack; suggestions.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2430"} 40:30
              You can try a direct analytical assault. If you are a young man and brave and you have a long way into the future, you can just get out your hammer and chisel and crack away.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2447"} 40:47
              Or you might think that you want something different.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2450"} 40:50
              You might find to get a better understanding of the formal structure of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              of this Yang-Mills type.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2458"} 40:58
              These Yang-Mills theories have very elaborate mathematical formal structure, which incorporate very mysterious
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+physics"} dualities
              of various kinds and also a thing called
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2475"} 41:51
              So here there is a very elaborate structure, and it is just possible that if we get a better understanding of that structure then that might have given us a better way of trying to lay the foundations.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2485"} 41:25
              For example, a problem that can be described in two different ways, by duality, two dual pictures might look quite different, one of them might be practical and the other one might be intractable.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2495"} 41:35
              They are certainly not at all unreasonable to think this might be a way starting to understand better these formal operators[?], the formal operators here is very sophisticated.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2507"} 41:47
              You could of course sit back and wait, await development in string theory.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2514"} 41:54
              String theory and the latest things that follow string theory, called
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
              and every other new theory, develop [at] an enormous rate.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2520"} 42:00
              The theoretical physics community here is tremendously active, there are very beautiful things happening every week, new results come out, many of them will have mathematical overtones.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2530"} 42:10
              And it could be that if we wait a few years there’ll be such a totally new picture emerging from string theory that we’ll get a better idea how to go about attacking the problem, so that’s not an unreasonable expectation.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2541"} 42:21
              You see, trying to lay the foundations for physics is like an architect trying to lay the foundations for a building that’s rapidly going up.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2550"} 42:30
              I mean, which do you do first, it’s not quite clear: You want to see the design or you want to lay the foundations. Maybe you want the design first.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2559"} 42:39
              Then, of course, you might also sit back and say: Well, quantum electrodynamics was very hard, because we didn’t incorporate extra things like proton and neutrons, we only worried about electrons. Maybe even that isn’t enough perhaps we should have incorporated gravity, then we’ll have all the forces under control and perhaps at that stage the fundamental theory will be even easier.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2580"} 43:00
              People in string theory aim here and this is the ultimate theory, and perhaps the ultimate theory will be easier than the transitional theories. But who knows.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/trxjCOmg8GY?t=2589"} 43:09
              The big challenge [for] mathematics and the physicists of the 21st century is to really make progress along this program by whatever method they can. Good luck to you.
          %p Early report on the progress (essentially none):
          %ul
            %li#Douglas04
