            discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            includes the following:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Klebanov"}> Igor Klebanov
                , Oyvind Tafjord, “Can we quantitatively understand quark and gluon confinement in Quantum Chromodynamics and the existence of a mass gap?”, 10th of
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/millennium.html"} 10 Physics Problems for the Next Millennium
                selected at
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/conferences/972466"} Strings 2000
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yakov+Shnir"}> Yakov Shnir
                , Section 9.1. of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Magnetic Monopoles
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.springer.com/gp/book/9783540252771"} ISBN:978-3-540-29082-7
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koji+Hashimoto"}> Koji Hashimoto
                , pp. 126 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em D-Brane – Superstrings and New Perspective of Our World
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-642-23574-0"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-23574-0
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/1188897"} spire:1188897
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#BarnafoldiGogokhia19"} Barnaföldi &amp; Gogokhia (2019), p. 2
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#RobertsSchmidt20"} Roberts &amp; Schmidt (2020), p. 2
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Roberts21"} Roberts (2021), footn. 2
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Roberts22"} Roberts (2022), p. 2
            %li
              %p
                The STAR Collaboration, ftn. 2 in:
                %em Probing QCD Confinement with Spin Entanglement
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.05499"}> arXiv:2506.05499
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %div
            %h3#OpenProblemConfinementReferences The open problem of confinement
            %p
              While
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
              as well as
              = succeed "-computer" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
              takes place, a real theoretical understanding has been missing (though
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-QCD"} AdS-QCD
              is now on a good track). This is the
              = succeed "." do
                %em confinement problem
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
              is called the
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+mass+gap"} Yang-Mills mass gap
                  %a{:href => "quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory#ReferencesNonperturbativeTheoryAndMassGap"} Millennium Problem
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "flavor+problem#TheFlavorProblem"} flavor problem
            %p The following is a list of quotes highlighting the open problem of confinement:
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Witten79
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Baryons in the
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/n
                    Expansion
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/140391"} spire:140391
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(79)90232-3"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(79)90232-3
            %blockquote
              %p
                many of the essential properties that the theory
                = succeed "QCD" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                is presumed to have, including confinement, dynamical mass generation, and chiral symmetry breaking, are only poorly understood. And apart from the low-lying bound states of heavy quarks, which we believe can be described by a nonrelativistic Schroedinger equation, we are unable to derive from the basic theory even the grossest features of the particle spectrum, or of traditional strong interaction phenomenology
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Machleidt89
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rupert+Machleidt"}> Rupert Machleidt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Meson Theory of Nuclear Forces and Nuclear Structure
                %em Advances in Nuclear Physics
                Advances in Nuclear Physics, vol 19. Springer 1989 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4613-9907-0_2"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4613-9907-0_2
                )
            %blockquote
              %p
                There are theoretical attempts to connect the fundamental theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                with the very successful
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meson"} meson
                picture at low energy. The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrme+model"} Skyrme model
                is an example. In other attempts, one tries to derive the NN interaction more or less directly from
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion"} pion
                and two-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion"} pion
                contributions have to be added by hand, as they do not emerge naturally out of
                = succeed "-inspired" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\pi
                are the most important parts of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+force"} nuclear force
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
                model calculations is serious.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Kutschke96
                Robert Kutschke, section 3.1
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Heavy flavour spectroscopy
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hadron Spectroscopy and the Confinement Problem
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.springer.com/cn/book/9780306453038"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4613-0375-6
            %blockquote
              %p While it is generally believed that QCD is the correct fundamental theory of the strong interactions there are, as yet, no practical means to produce full QCD calculations of hadron masses and their decay widths.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Holstein99
                Barry R. Holstein,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Brief Introduction to Chiral Perturbation Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9911449"} arXiv:hep-ph/9911449
            %blockquote
              %p the holy grail sought by particle/nuclear knights has been to verify the correctness of the “ultimate” theory of strong interactions – quantum chromodynamics (QCD).
              %p
                The theory is, of course, deceptively simple on the surface.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
                So why are we still not satisfied? While at the very largest energies, asymptotic freedom allows the use of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative techniques
              %p
                i) QCD is written in terms of the “wrong” degrees of freedom –
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluons"} gluons
                – while low energy experiments are performed with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadronic
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
              %p
                ii) the theory is non-linear due to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluon"} gluon
                self-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"}> interactions
                ;
              %p
                iii) the theory is one of strong coupling so that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative methods
                are not practical
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Klebanov"}> Igor Klebanov
                , Oyvind Tafjord, “Can we quantitatively understand quark and gluon confinement in Quantum Chromodynamics and the existence of a mass gap?”, 10th of
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/millennium.html"} 10 Physics Problems for the Next Millennium
                selected at
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/conferences/972466"} Strings 2000
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yakov+Shnir"}> Yakov Shnir
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Magnetic Monopoles
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://www.springer.com/gp/book/9783540252771"} ISBN:978-3-540-29082-7
                %p Section 9.1:
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    confinement
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                    still remains one of the very few Big Unsolved Problems of the theoretical physics of the XXI-st century. Moreover, this is the “classic question that has resisted solution over the years”, which was included by the Clay Mathematics Institute in the list of seven Millennium Prize Problems
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a{:href => "mass+gap#JaffeWitten00"}
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %mn 14
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [14]
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#CsakiReece06
                %p
                  Csaba Csaki,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Reece"} Matthew Reece
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Toward a Systematic Holographic QCD: A Braneless Approach
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0608266"} arxiv:hep-ph/0608266
                %p
                  (in motivation of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FQCD"} AdS/QCD
            %blockquote
              %p QCD is a perennially problematic theory. Despite its decades of experimental support, the detailed low-energy physics remains beyond our calculational reach. The lattice provides a technique for answering nonperturbative questions, but to date there remain open questions that have not been answered.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Guidry08
                %p
                  Mike Guidry,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Gauge Field Theories: An Introduction with Applications
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://www.wiley.com/en-us/Gauge+Field+Theories%3A+An+Introduction+with+Applications-p-9783527617364"} ISBN:978-3-527-61736-4
                %p Section 13.1.9:
            %blockquote
              %p The holy grail of QCD is the proof that a color SU(3) gauge theory confines in the non-perturbative regime.
              %p This is not difficult to show for lattices with large spacing; unfortunately, such a demonstration does not constitute a proof of QCD confinement: to do that we must also demonstrate that the same theory that confines at large lattice spacing (strong coupling) has a continuum limit (weak coupling) that is consistent with the asymptotically free short distance behavior of QCD.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Yi09
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Piljin+Yi"}> Piljin Yi
                ,
                %em Holographic Baryons
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.4515"}> arXiv:0902.4515
                ,
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.worldscientific.com/doi/abs/10.1142/9789814280709_0016"} doi:10.1142/9789814280709_0016
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mannque+Rho"} Mannque Rho
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ismail+Zahed"} Ismail Zahed
                (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Multifaceted+Skyrmion"} The Multifaceted Skyrmion
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9710"} doi:10.1142/9710
            %blockquote
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                is a challenging theory. Its most interesting aspects, namely the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color
                and the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+symmetry+breaking"} chiral symmetry breaking
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                below the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#HashimotoIizukaYi10
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koji+Hashimoto"}> Koji Hashimoto
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Norihiro+Iizuka"} Norihiro Iizuka
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Piljin+Yi"} Piljin Yi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Matrix Model for Baryons and Nuclear Forces
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.4988"} arXiv:1003.4988
            %blockquote
              %p
                Nuclear physics is one of the oldest branches of high energy physics, yet remains one of more difficult. Despite the fact that we know the underlying fundamental theory, i.e. QCD, we are still unable to predict, reliably and analytically, behavior of nuclei or even a single proton. The problem is of course that one must understand the strong-coupling regime of QCD, which by and large remains inaccessible except by large-scale lattice simulations. Traditionally, this sets nuclear physics apart from the rest of high energy physics in many aspects. However,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto+model"} recent developments
                in the so-called
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT"} gauge/gravity duality
                began to solve certain strongly coupled field theories,possibly including QCD or its close relatives, allowing the two communities to merge with each other.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Goldstein11
                Jeff Greensite,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to the Confinement Problem
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-642-14382-3"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-14382-3
            %blockquote
              %p Because of the great importance of the standard model, and the central role it plays in our understanding of particle physics, it is unfortunate that, in one very important respect, we don’t really understand how it works. The problem lies in the sector dealing with the interactions of quarks and gluons, the sector known as Quantum Chromodynamics or QCD. We simply do not know for sure why quarks and gluons, which are the fundamental fields of the theory, don’t show up in the actual spectrum of the theory, as asymptotic particle states. There is wide agreement about what must be happening in high energy particle collisions: the formation of color electric flux tubes among quarks and antiquarks, and the eventual fragmentation of those flux tubes into mesons and baryons, rather than free quarks and gluons. But there is no general agreement about why this is happening, and that limitation exposes our general ignorance about the workings of non-abelian gauge theories in general, and QCD in particular, at large distance scales.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#MachleidtEntem11
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rupert+Machleidt"}> Rupert Machleidt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Rodr%C3%ADguez+Entem"} David Rodríguez Entem
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Chiral effective field theory and nuclear forces
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.2919"} arXiv:1105.2919
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physrep.2011.02.001"} doi:10.1016/j.physrep.2011.02.001
            %blockquote
              %p
                The problem with a derivation of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+forces"} nuclear forces
                from
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                is that this theory is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+QFT"} non-perturbative
                in the low-energy regime characteristic of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+physics"} nuclear physics
                = succeed "-inspired" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
                models became popular. The positive aspect of these models is that they try to explain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
                structure and hadron-hadron
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
                on an equal footing and, indeed, some of the gross features of the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleon"} nucleon
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleon"} nucleon
                interaction are explained successfully.
              %p
                However, on a critical note, it must be pointed out that these quark-based approaches are nothing but another set of models and, thus, do not represent fundamental progress. For the purpose of describing hadron-hadron interactions, one may equally well stay with the simpler and much more quantitative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meson"} meson
                models.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koji+Hashimoto"}> Koji Hashimoto
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em D-Brane – Superstrings and New Perspective of Our World
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-642-23574-0"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-23574-0
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/1188897"} spire:1188897
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p pp 126: Heuristically, one may speculate that, since the coupling constant of QCD is too large, quarks bind together so that any single quark may not be able to exist alone. However, it has never been proved theoretically. After all, at present we have not understood yet QCD, the theory with a large coupling constant in which quantum theoretical effects (many loops in Feynman graphs as in Fig. 2.18) are essential. This is really a serious problem. Actually, one of big seven mathematical millennium problems proposed by Clay mathematical institute in the United States is concerning this QCD, and one million dollars will be awarded for a solution of the problem related with this.
              %p D-branes give us quite an innovative idea, as an approach to this problem. It is called “holography” which is introduced in this chapter.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Brambilla14
                Brambilla et al.:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD+and+strongly+coupled+gauge+theories+-+challenges+and+perspectives"} QCD and strongly coupled gauge theories - challenges and perspectives
                %strong 74
                10 (2014) 2981 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1140/epjc/s10052-014-2981-5"}> doi:10.1140/epjc/s10052-014-2981-5
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.3723"} arXiv:1404.3723
            %blockquote
              %p
                The success of the technique does not remove the challenge of understanding the non-perturbative aspects of the theory. The two aspects are deeply intertwined. The Lagrangian of QCD is written in terms of quark and gluon degrees of freedom which become apparent at large energy but remain hidden inside hadrons in the low-energy regime. This confinement property is related to the increase of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_s
                at low energy, but it has never been demonstrated analytically.
              %p We have clear indications of the confinement of quarks into hadrons from both experiments and lattice QCD. Computations of the heavy quark–antiquark potential, for example, display a linear behavior in the quark–antiquark distance, which cannot be obtained in pure perturbation theory. Indeed the two main characteristics of QCD: confinement and the appearance of nearly massless pseudoscalar mesons, emergent from the spontaneous breaking of chiral symmetry, are non-perturbative phenomena whose precise understanding continues to be a target of research.
              %p Even in the simpler case of gluodynamics in the absence of quarks, we do not have a precise understanding of how a gap in the spectrum is formed and the glueball spectrum is generated.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#INFN15
                %a{:href => "https://web.infn.it/csn1/index.php/en/"}> Istituto Nazionale di Fisica Nucleare
                ,
                %em What Next: White Paper of the INFN-CSN1
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Proposal for a long term strategy for accelerator based experiments
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/1374543/"} spire:1374543
                %a{:href => "http://www.lnf.infn.it/sis/frascatiseries/Volume60/Volume60.pdf"} pdf
                ) chapter 7:
                %em Hadron Physics and non-perturbative QCD
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.infn.it/csn1/White_paper_documents/NPQCD.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %blockquote
              %p Experimentally, there is a large number of facts that lack a detailed qualitative and quantitative explanation. The most spectacular manifestation of our lack of theoretical understanding of QCD is the failure to observe the elementary degrees of freedom, quarks and gluons, as free asymptotic states (color con- finement) and the occurrence, instead, of families of massive mesons and baryons (hadrons) that form approximately linear Regge trajectories in the mass squared. The internal, partonic structure of hadrons, and nucleons in particular, is still largely mysterious. Since protons and neutrons form almost all the visible matter of the Universe, it is of basic importance to explore their static and dynamical properties in terms of quarks and gluons interacting according to QCD dynamics.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#CobosMartinez16
                J J Cobos-Martínez,
                %em Non-perturbative QCD and hadron physics
                2016 J. Phys.: Conf. Ser. 761 012036 (
                %a{:href => "http://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1742-6596/761/1/012036"}> doi:10.1088/1742-6596/761/1/012036
                )
            %blockquote
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
                the QCD Lagrangian does not by itself explain the data on strongly interacting matter, and it is not clear how the observed bound states, the hadrons, and their properties arise from QCD. Neither confinement nor dynamical chiral symmetry breaking (DCSB) is apparent in QCD’s lagrangian, yet they play a dominant role in determining the observable characteristics of QCD. The physics of strongly interacting matter is governed by emergent phenomena such as these, which can only be elucidated through the use of non-perturbative methods in QCD [4, 5, 6, 7]
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#SNM16
                Hideo Suganuma, Yuya Nakagawa, Kohei Matsumoto,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    1+1 Large
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c
                    QCD and its Holographic Dual -Soliton Picture of Baryons in Single-Flavor World
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02074"} arxiv:1610.02074
            %blockquote
              %p Since 1973, quantum chromodynamics (QCD) has been established as the fundamental theory of the strong interaction. Nevertheless, it is very difficult to solve QCD directly in an analytical manner, and many effective models of QCD have been used instead of QCD, but most models cannot be derived from QCD and its connection to QCD is unclear. To analyze nonperturbative QCD, the lattice QCD Monte Carlo simulation has been also used as a first-principle calculation of the strong interaction. However, it has several weak points. For example, the state information (e.g. the wave function) is severely limited, because lattice QCD is based on the path-integral formalism. Also, it is difficult to take the chiral limit, because zero-mass pions require infinite volume lattices. There appears a notorious “sign problem” at finite density.
              %p
                On the other hand,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
                has a direct connection to QCD, and can be derived from QCD in some limit. In fact,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
                is equivalent to infrared QCD in large
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c
                and strong ‘t Hooft coupling
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+meson+dominance"} vector meson dominance
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrme soliton
                picture. Unlike
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+QCD"} lattice QCD
                simulations,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
                is usually formulated in the chiral limit, and does not have the sign problem at finite density.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Petrov19
                V. A. Petrov,
                %em Asymptotic Regimes of Hadron Scattering in QCD
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.02628"}> arXiv:1901.02628
                )
            %blockquote
              %p
                This is a commonplace that so far we do not have a full-fledged theory of interaction of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadrons"} hadrons
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                and having a regular way of calculating of hadronic
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} amplitudes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} colour confinement
                with massive colourless physical states (hadrons).
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#HashimotoMatsuoMorita19
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koji+Hashimoto"}> Koji Hashimoto
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoshinori+Matsuo"} Yoshinori Matsuo
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Takeshi+Morita"} Takeshi Morita
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Nuclear states and spectra in holographic QCD
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.07444"} arXiv:1902.07444
            %blockquote
              %p One of the long-standing problems in QCD is to reproduce profound nuclear physics. The strong coupling nature of QCD prevents us from solving it analytically, and even numerical simulations have a limitation such as the volume of the atomic nucleus versus the lattice size. It is quite important to bridge the particle physics and the nuclear physics, by solving QCD to derive typical fundamental notions of the nuclear physics, such as the magic numbers, the nuclear binding energy and the nuclear shell model.
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} Holographic QCD
                is an analytic method to approach these problems in the strong coupling limit and at a large
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+matrix+model"} nuclear matrix model
                is a many-body quantum effective mechanics for multiple baryons, derived by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
                applied to QCD.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#BarnafoldiGogokhia19
                Gergely Gábor Barnaföldi, Vakhtang Gogokhia,
                %em The Mass Gap Approach to QCD
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.07748"}> arXiv:1904.07748
                )
            %blockquote
              %p
                QCD as a fundamental quantum field gauge theory still suffers from a few important conceptual problems. We focus on some of them as follows: (A) The dynamical generation of a mass squared at the fundamental quark-gluon level, since the QCD Lagrangianforbids such kind of terms apart from the current quark mass.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\ldots]
                (D) The non-observation of the colored objects as physical states which does not follow from the QCD Lagrangian,i.e., it cannot explain confinement of gluons and quarks.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#HKL19
                Yonggoo Heo, C. Kobdaj, M.F.M. Lutz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Constraints from a large-
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c
                    analysis on meson-baryon interactions at chiral order
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Q
                            %mn 3
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q^3
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.11816"} arXiv:1908.11816
            %blockquote
              %p Still after many decades of vigorous studies the outstanding challenge of modern physics is to establish a rigorous link of QCD to low-energy hadron physics as it is observed in the many experimental cross section measurements.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#DHMMNVW19
                Christian Drischler, Wick Haxton, Kenneth McElvain, Emanuele Mereghetti, Amy Nicholson, Pavlos Vranas, André Walker-Loud,
                %em Towards grounding nuclear physics in QCD
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07961"}> arxiv:1910.07961
                )
            %blockquote
              %p
                the entirety of the rich field of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+physics"} nuclear physics
                emerges from
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
                binding
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/protons"} protons
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrons"} neutrons
                into the nuclear landscape, to the fusion and fission reactions between
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nucleus"} nuclei
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+of+matter"} state of matter
                at the cores of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutron+stars"} neutron stars
              %p
                How does this emergence take place exactly? How is the clustering of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
                into
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleons"} nucleons
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alpha+particles"} alpha particles
                realized? What are the mechanisms behind collective phenomena in nuclei as strongly correlated many-body systems? How does the extreme fine-tuning required to reproduce nuclear binding energies proceed? – are big open questions in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+physics"} nuclear physics
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#RobertsSchmidt20
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Craig+Roberts"}> Craig Roberts
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sebastian+M.+Schmidt"} Sebastian M. Schmidt
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Reflections upon the Emergence of Hadronic Mass
                %strong 229
                (2020) 3319–3340 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1140/epjst/e2020-000064-6"}> doi:10.1140/epjst/e2020-000064-6
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.08782"} arXiv:2006.08782
            %blockquote
              %p
                More than 98% of visible mass is contained within
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nuclei"} nuclei
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrons"} neutrons
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/protons"} protons
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleons"}> nucleons
                ) they contain. Each nucleon has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi N
                      %mo ∼
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_N \sim 1
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
                mass. The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+boson"} Higgs boson
                produces the latter, but what produces the masses of the neutron and proton? This is the question posed above, which is pivotal to the development of modern physics: how can science explain the emergence of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadronic
                mass (EHM)?
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
              %p
                Modern science is thus encumbered with the fundamental problem of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluon"} gluon
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton"} proton
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
                Without confinement,our Universe cannot exist.
              %p
                As the 21st Century began, the Clay Mathematics Institute established seven Millennium Prize Problems. Each represents one of the toughest challenges in mathematics. The set contains the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+mass+gap"} problem of confinement
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
                = succeed "." do
                  %em grand challenge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
              %p
                In trying to match
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                with Nature, one confronts the many complexities of strong, nonlinear dynamics in relativistic quantum field theory, e.g. the loss of particle number conservation, the frame and scale dependence of the explanations and interpretations of observable processes, and the evolving character of the relevant degrees-of-freedom.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+field"} Electroweak theory
                and phenomena are essentially
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                such as that at work in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Chatterjee20
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sourav+Chatterjee"}> Sourav Chatterjee
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A probabilistic mechanism for quark confinement
                %strong 385
                (2021) 1007–1039 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-021-04086-y"}> doi:10.1007/s00220-021-04086-y
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16229"} arXiv:2006.16229
            %blockquote
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
                is one of the enduring mysteries ofmodern physics. [\ldots] In spite of many decades of research, physically relevant quantum gauge theories have not yet been constructed in a rigorous mathematical sense.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbatively
                = succeed "]" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi …
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SU%283%29"} SU(3)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
                at all values of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling strength
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/glue-balls"} glue-balls
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} theory of strong interactions
                have
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
              %p
                The second big open question is the problem of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                Michael Creutz,
                %em QCD beyond diagrams
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.04774"}> arXiv:2010.04774
                )
            %blockquote
              %p QCD, the theory of the strong interactions, involves quarks interacting with non-Abelian gluon fields. This theory has many features that are difficult to impossible to see in conventional diagrammatic perturbation theory. This includes quark confinement, mass generation, and chiral symmetry breaking.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Roberts21
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Craig+Roberts"}> Craig Roberts
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On Mass and Matter
                %strong 31
                6 (2021) [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s43673-021-00005-4"}> doi:10.1007/s43673-021-00005-4
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.08340"} arXiv:2101.08340
            %blockquote
              %p The origin of the proton mass, and with it the basic mass-scale for all nuclear physics, is one of the most profound puzzles in Nature.
              %p Although QCD is defined by a seemingly simple Lagrangian, it specifies a problem that has defied solution for more than forty years. The key challenges in modern nuclear and high-energy physics are to reveal the observable content of strong QCD and, ultimately, therefrom derive the properties of nuclei.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guy+de+Teramond"}> Guy de Teramond
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stanley+Brodsky"} Stanley Brodsky
                %em Longitudinal dynamics and chiral symmetry breaking in holographic light-front QCD
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.10950"}> arXiv:2103.10950
                )
            %blockquote
              %p In spite of the important progress of Euclidean lattice gauge theory, a basic understanding of the mechanism of color confinement and its relation to chiral symmetry breaking in QCD has remained an unsolved problem.
              %p
                Recent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+light+front+QCD"} developments
                based on superconformal quantum mechanics in light-front quantization and its holographic embedding on a higher dimensional gravity theory (gauge/gravity correspondence) have led to new analytic insights into the structure of hadrons and their dynamics.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Klebanov21
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Klebanov"}> Igor Klebanov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Remarks on Color Confinement
                %a{:href => "https://www.ictp-saifr.org/strings2021/"} Strings 2021
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.ictp-saifr.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/DiscussionConfinementKlebanov.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/B9NI2LE7d68?t=262"} video
            %blockquote
              %p
                Perhaps the gauge/string duality has provided us with a “physicist’s proof of confinement” in some exotic gauge theories like the one described by the warped deformed conifold. Yet, we still don’t have a quantitative handle on the Asymptotically Free theories in 3+1 dimensions.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
                Don’t take confinement for granted, even in 1+1 dimensions where it seems obvious. Proof of Color Confinement in 2+1 and 3+1 dimensions would be very important.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Dubovsky21
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+Dubovsky"}> Sergei Dubovsky
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Comments on (mostly long) QCD strings
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ictp-saifr.org/strings2021/"} Strings 2021
                  (
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ictp-saifr.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/Sergei.pdf"}> pdf
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Dubovsky_QCDStrings.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/B9NI2LE7d68?t=1183"} video
                %p
                  concludes in view of
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FQCD"} AdS/QCD
                %blockquote
                  %p With modern lattice tools and understanding of QFT/string theory we are in a good position to solve this problem. It may take another 30 years to build a new collider and get new data in particle physics. But we are lucky to have a tractable and fundamental problem to solve while waiting. Participants of STRINGS 2051 will be puzzled if we don’t make an effort now and leave it to them.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  Clara Peset, Antonio Pineda, Oleksandr Tomalak,
                  %em The proton radius (puzzle?) and its relatives
                  (
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.00695"}> arXiv:2106.00695
                  )
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    (in the context of the
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton+radius+puzzle"} proton radius puzzle
            %blockquote
              %p In the beginning God created quarks, And made them interact through the strong forces, And it was dark… And God said, “I do not understand a damn thing”
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                Sophia K Domokos, Robert Bell, Trinh La, Patrick Mazza,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Pedagogical Introduction to Holographic Hadrons
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Holographic hadron masses in the language of quantum mechanics
                %strong 42
                6 (2021) 065801
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.13136"} arXiv:2106.13136
                %a{:href => "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1361-6404/ac1abb"} doi:10.1088/1361-6404/ac1abb
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p We have known for over fifty years that protons are made up of quarks and gluons. We have pinned down the masses and couplings of quarks to a startling degree of accuracy. Yet we still don’t know why the proton’s mass is almost exactly a factor of 100 greater than the sum of the masses of its constituent quarks. This mystery persists because quarks and gluons are strongly coupled at low energies: they interact so forcefully and often that our usual calculational tools – based almost entirely on perturbation theory – fail.
              %p
                Holographic duality, or “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"}> holographic QCD
                ” (hQCD) when applied to hadrons, emerged in the early 2000s as a new way to tackle strongly coupled systems.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                Roman Pasechnik, Michal Šumbera,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Different faces of confinement
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07600"} arXiv:2109.07600
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.3390/universe7090330"} doi:10.3390/universe7090330
            %blockquote
              %p in the infrared (IR) limit, QCD enters entirely different, strongly-coupled domain, rendering the Perturbation Theory inapplicable and creating substantial problems for making reliable predictions at intermediate and low momentum transfers, i.e. at large distances. While it is conventionally believed that QCD should remain the correct theory of strong interactions also at large distances, in the so-called confined regime, deriving reliable predictions remains a big theoretical challenge.
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
              %p
                The
                %em problem of confinement
                concerns the strongly-coupled sector of QCD composed of interacting colored partons (quarks and gluons).
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
                Despite the major efforts of the research community and tremendous progress made over last few decades, it does not appear to be fully and consistently resolved yet.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li#Koppenburg21
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Patrick+Koppenburg"} Patrick Koppenburg
                for the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHCb+collaboration"} LHCb collaboration
                %em List of hadrons observed at the LHC
                (2021)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://cds.cern.ch/record/2749030/files/FIGURE-2021-001.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.nikhef.nl/~pkoppenb/particles.html"} webpage
                %a{:href => "https://cds.cern.ch/record/2749030"} cds:2749030
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p While the LHC is best known for the observation of the first fundamental scalar particle, the Higgs Boson, it has also yielded many observations of yet unknown hadrons. Hadrons are composed of quarks and are thus not fundamental particles of the Standard Model. However, their properties follow from yet unsolved mysteries of the strong interaction.
              %p Although the QCD Lagrangian is expected to completely describe the spectrum of hadrons and all of their properties, there is no rigorous first-principle way of expressing this mathematically. The quark confinement conjecture is experimentally well tested, but mathematically still unproven. And it is still unknown which combinations of quarks may or may not form hadrons. Experimental guidance is needed to help improving theoretical models.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                Daniele Binosi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Emergent Hadron Mass in Strong Dynamics
                %strong 63
                42 (2022)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.00942"} arXiv:2203.00942
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00601-022-01740-6"} doi:10.1007/s00601-022-01740-6
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+freedom"} asymptotic freedom
                implies that the theory is innately
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonperturbative+quantum+field+theory"} nonperturbative
                at short distances, which in turn means that it is extremely difficult to describe
                = succeed "\u2019s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fm"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi fm
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fm
                scale. Indeed, QCD’s colour-charged degrees of freedom, the massless gluons and the light up and down quarks, have never been observed in isolation: they are confined in colour-neutral bound-states, the hadrons, of which the (light) pion and the (heavy) proton are primary examples. It should be noticed that without a mass-scale confinement would not be possible: colour-singlet combinations of quarks would still be there, but the participating particles would need not be close together, since in a scale invariant theory all lengths are equivalent. Accordingly, the question “how does confinement appear in QCD” is inextricably connected to the question “how does mass emerge in strong dynamics”.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Shifman"}> Mikhail Shifman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em OPE-based Methods in Nonperturbative QCD
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.10600"} arXiv:2208.10600
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %blockquote
              %p
                QCD and its relatives are special because QCD is the theory of nature. QCD is strongly coupled in the infrared domain where it is impossible to treat it quasi-classically – perturbation theory fails even qualitatively. It does not capture drastic rearrangement of the vacuum structure related to confinement. The Lagrangian is defined at short distances in terms of gluons and quarks, while at large distances we deal with
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadrons"} hadrons
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} Non-perturbative methods
                were desperately needed.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  Minghui Ding,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Craig+D.+Roberts"} Craig D. Roberts
                  %em Emergence of Hadron Mass and Structure
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.07763"} arXiv:2211.07763
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %li#Roberts22
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Craig+D.+Roberts"}> Craig D. Roberts
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Origin of the Proton Mass
                  %em 8th International Symposium on Symmetries in Subatomic Physics (SSP 2022)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09905"} arXiv:2211.09905
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1051/epjconf/202328201006"} doi:10.1051/epjconf/202328201006
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p (see the articles for a wealth of relevant commentary)
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guy+de+T%C3%A9ramond"}> Guy de Téramond
                  ,
                  %em Emergent phenomena in QCD: The holographic perspective
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.14028"} arXiv:2212.14028
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    (discussion in
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+light+front+QCD"} holographic light front QCD
            %blockquote
              %p At large distances, however, the nonperturbative nature of the strong interactions becomes dominant and a basic understanding of the essential features of hadron physics from first principles QCD has remained an important unsolved problem in the standard model of particle physics. Hadronic characteristics are not explicit properties of the QCD Lagrangian but emergent phenomena, notably, the origin of the hadron mass scale, the mechanism of color confinement, the relation between chiral symmetry breaking and confinement, the massless pion in the chiral limit (the limit of zero quark masses), bound states and the pattern of hadron excitations.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                Seth Grable,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Romatschke"} Paul Romatschke
                %em Elements of Confinement for QCD with Twelve Massless Quarks
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12203"} arXiv:2310.12203
            %blockquote
              %p
                Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) is notorious for being a quantum field theory that is difficult to solve on large length scales
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\ldots]
                what is missing in current QCD literature is a (potentially approximate) solution to continuum QCD that captures the main qualitative features such as confinement in the IR and asymptotic freedom in the UV
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\ldots]
                if such a solution does exist, it cannot be based on a perturbative weak-coupling expansion, because QCD becomes strongly coupled towards long length scales. So the putative solution must arise from non-perturbative methods.
            %p
              %br/
            %ul
              %li
                The STAR Collaboration:
                %em Probing QCD Confinement with Spin Entanglement
                [
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.05499"}> arXiv:2506.05499
                ]
            %blockquote
              %p
                The fundamental and
                %a{:href => "Yang-Mills+mass+gap#ReferencesMassGapProblem"} literally million-dollar question
                is to understand the origin of these emergent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
                structures, e.g.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                [13], the theory of strong interactions, predicts that the strong force grows as the distance between partons increases, offering a qualitative explanation for confinement. However, as the distance scale approaches the typical length scale of a hadron, ∼ 1
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fm"} fm
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 10
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 15
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{-15}
                meters), the complexity of first-principle QCD calculations becomes difficult to solve numerically at current computational power, due to the nature of low energy self-interacting gluons. This challenging regime, where simple approximations no longer work, is called nonperturbative QCD. Therefore, the detailed mechanisms through which confinement occurs from partons to hadrons, and how it manifests itself in hadron structure, remain unresolved puzzles [14].
            %p
              %br/
          %h3#approaches Approaches
          %h4#computer_lattice_qft Computer lattice QFT
          %p
            Of course various partial approaches exist, notably computer-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+QCD"} lattice QCD
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis"} Riemann hypothesis
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "Riemann+hypothesis#ReferencesComputerChecks"} there
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/masses"} masses
            in
            = succeed "-simulations" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+QCD"} lattice QCD
          %ul
            %li
              %p S. Durr, Z. Fodor, J. Frison, C. Hoelbling, R. Hoffmann, S.D. Katz, S. Krieg, T. Kurth, L. Lellouch, T. Lippert, K.K. Szabo, G. Vulvert,
              %p
                %em> Ab-initio Determination of Light Hadron Masses
                ,
              %p
                Science 322:1224-1227,2008 (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.3599"}> arXiv:0906.3599
                )
            %li
              %p
                Zoltan Fodor, Christian Hoelbling, section V of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Light Hadron Masses from Lattice QCD
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.4789"} arXiv:1203.4789
            %li
              %p
                S. Aoki et. al.
                %em Review of lattice results concerning low-energy particle physics
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00299"}> arXiv:1607.00299
                )
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li
              Yonggoo Heo, C. Kobdaj, M.F.M. Lutz,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Constraints from a large-
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c
                  analysis on meson-baryon interactions at chiral order
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Q
                          %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q^3
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.11816"} arXiv:1908.11816
          %blockquote
            %p Still after many decades of vigorous studies the outstanding challenge of modern physics is to establish a rigorous link of QCD to low-energy hadron physics as it is observed in the many experimental cross section measurements.
          %h4#rigorous_lattice_qft Rigorous lattice QFT
          %p
            But there is also an approach via rigorous analytic
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
          %ul
            %li#Chatterjee20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sourav+Chatterjee"}> Sourav Chatterjee
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A probabilistic mechanism for quark confinement
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16229"} arXiv:2006.16229
          %p (…)
          %h3#in_solid_state_physics In solid state physics
          %p
            Discussion in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
          %ul
            %li
              Alessandra N. Braga, Wagner P. Pires, Jeferson Danilo L. Silva, Danilo T. Alves, Van Sérgio Alves,
